WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.855 --> 00:00:04.674
A user story says what a user wants, in their own words.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.880
The template is Connextra's, around 2001: as a who, I want what, so that why.

3
00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:17.200
As an operator on line three, I want to pick a cause in two taps,

4
00:00:17.327 --> 00:00:19.870
so the shift leader knows where the time went.

5
00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:23.432
That so that matters most: it says why.

6
00:00:24.488 --> 00:00:30.003
Ron Jeffries of Extreme Programming described the three Cs in 2001: Card,

7
00:00:30.165 --> 00:00:32.081
Conversation and Confirmation.

8
00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:37.857
A card as a reminder, details from a conversation, agreed checks: a promise,

9
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.053
not a contract.

10
00:00:40.458 --> 00:00:44.044
Acceptance criteria are concrete, testable conditions.

11
00:00:45.073 --> 00:00:48.301
Here: at most eight causes, with work gloves on,

12
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.275
at the shift leader within a minute.

13
00:00:51.346 --> 00:00:55.073
Without criteria, the argument only starts at delivery.

14
00:00:56.084 --> 00:01:00.078
An epic is too big for one sprint, like OEE per line.

15
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.836
You cut it later.

16
00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:08.261
Stories and epics are Product Backlog items; the Scrum Guide prescribes no format.

17
00:01:09.328 --> 00:01:13.728
Mistakes: a task as a story, no so that, or the user as the role.

18
00:01:14.734 --> 00:01:18.322
Ask of every story: who notices when this is done, and how?

19
00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:24.064
A planner at a steel-door maker hands in thirty pages of requirements.

20
00:01:25.073 --> 00:01:27.720
Page four: show the load on all machines.

21
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:30.030
Who needs that, and what for?

22
00:01:31.052 --> 00:01:32.178
After ten minutes:

23
00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:36.718
as a planner I want each welding robot's load for five working days,

24
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.841
so I can put rush orders on a robot with room.

25
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:46.371
Criteria: hours per day, red above ninety percent, refreshed every fifteen minutes.

26
00:01:47.372 --> 00:01:52.678
Thirty pages become about twenty-five stories; six pages deliver nothing to anyone.

27
00:01:54.037 --> 00:01:58.031
Refinement is ongoing work on the Product Backlog: cutting items,

28
00:01:58.205 --> 00:02:00.806
adding details and criteria, sizing.

29
00:02:01.863 --> 00:02:05.671
Not a separate event; often a fixed slot of an hour or two a week.

30
00:02:06.691 --> 00:02:11.579
The Developers who do the work size it; the Product Owner explains what and why.

31
00:02:12.609 --> 00:02:16.789
A known checklist is INVEST, by Bill Wake, 2003.

32
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:21.842
Independent, negotiable, valuable, estimable, small, testable.

33
00:02:22.872 --> 00:02:25.623
A weak letter tells you what to do in refinement.

34
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.038
The hardest part is story splitting:

35
00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:31.987
smaller stories that each deliver something working.

36
00:02:33.023 --> 00:02:38.747
Not horizontally into layers, database, logic, screen: then the user gets nothing.

37
00:02:39.789 --> 00:02:44.572
Vertically, like slices of cake: by step, rule, line or product type.

38
00:02:45.292 --> 00:02:46.604
The simplest first.

39
00:02:47.675 --> 00:02:51.471
A Definition of Ready says when a story is ready for a sprint.

40
00:02:52.215 --> 00:02:55.083
It is not in the Scrum Guide; don't make it a gate.

41
00:02:56.142 --> 00:03:00.565
Ordering is the Product Owner's job: by value, risk and dependencies.

42
00:03:01.865 --> 00:03:06.672
For you as a Lean consultant: refinement is work preparation; without criteria,

43
00:03:06.834 --> 00:03:08.053
a story gets stuck.

44
00:03:09.151 --> 00:03:14.155
Mistakes: refining far ahead, or not at all, so Sprint Planning takes hours.

45
00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.020
Example: window frames.

46
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.261
As a planner I want orders spread over the saws automatically,

47
00:03:21.377 --> 00:03:23.176
so I don't puzzle an hour a day.

48
00:03:24.164 --> 00:03:26.242
The team estimates three sprints.

49
00:03:26.967 --> 00:03:28.221
So you cut vertically.

50
00:03:29.198 --> 00:03:35.085
First one saw, standard profiles only; then all three, rush orders, changeover time.

51
00:03:36.104 --> 00:03:39.332
Story one fits a sprint: in use after two weeks,

52
00:03:39.506 --> 00:03:42.083
and the team learns whether the rules are right.

53
00:03:43.437 --> 00:03:46.177
People are bad at hours but fair at comparing.

54
00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:48.793
That is relative estimation.

55
00:03:49.838 --> 00:03:54.308
Story points give the size: work, complexity and uncertainty together.

56
00:03:55.042 --> 00:03:57.341
An eight is about four times a two.

57
00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.516
Points are per team: A's five is not B's five.

58
00:04:02.496 --> 00:04:06.560
Teams pick from a modified Fibonacci sequence: the gaps grow.

59
00:04:07.558 --> 00:04:09.358
Torn between eight and thirteen?

60
00:04:10.023 --> 00:04:12.182
Big and uncertain: maybe cut it.

61
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:15.351
Points began as ideal days.

62
00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:18.955
Ron Jeffries wrote in 2019,

63
00:04:19.106 --> 00:04:22.473
half joking, that he may have invented them and is sorry.

64
00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:29.835
Planning poker, described by James Grenning in 2002: estimating together with cards.

65
00:04:30.823 --> 00:04:35.537
The Product Owner reads a story; all pick silently and turn over at once.

66
00:04:36.529 --> 00:04:37.574
Three and thirteen?

67
00:04:38.281 --> 00:04:42.368
Highest and lowest explain why: that conversation is the value.

68
00:04:43.061 --> 00:04:44.129
Then another round.

69
00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:49.777
Velocity is the points a team finishes per sprint, by the Definition of Done.

70
00:04:50.795 --> 00:04:54.986
Use the spread: a team doing eighteen to thirty is not planned at thirty.

71
00:04:55.985 --> 00:05:00.003
Not a productivity measure: make it a target, and estimates grow.

72
00:05:00.998 --> 00:05:02.542
Some just count items.

73
00:05:03.275 --> 00:05:04.900
Ask: what are these points for?

74
00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:09.384
Tow bars: six sprints of eighteen to thirty points,

75
00:05:09.524 --> 00:05:11.776
twenty-three point three on average.

76
00:05:12.815 --> 00:05:15.021
A hundred and twenty points left.

77
00:05:15.775 --> 00:05:18.085
The sales director asks: when is it done?

78
00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:22.393
Six sprints, says the average: a false certainty.

79
00:05:23.396 --> 00:05:25.788
At the fastest sprint: four sprints.

80
00:05:26.513 --> 00:05:29.230
At the slowest: six point seven, so seven.

81
00:05:30.255 --> 00:05:34.388
Four to seven sprints: eight to fourteen weeks, most likely twelve.

82
00:05:35.393 --> 00:05:39.201
Only if the list doesn't grow, and it nearly always does.

83
00:05:39.924 --> 00:05:41.828
Update the range every sprint.
